tially infected they're often  challenged with  a wide collection of genetically related  viruses  however through selection pressure often  is only a single virus that produces  the infection within the individual  during the time in which they're  infected but don't know it  the virus will rapidly replicate however  the error-prone process  of its reverse transcriptase causes  a genetic diversity to emerge within  this population  that expands over the course of years  now once they're on therapy the amount  of circulating virus can  go down to undetectable levels and even  if with our most sensitive methods we  often only see a  single circulating variant within the  population  however we know that in these anatomical  and cellular reservoirs  there's a collection of latent virus  there's the entire history  that this patient has been exposed to  now there are many parts of hiv that we  can explore  but today i want to just talk about  one viral protein called tat now  here i've shown the viral diversity of a  large  collection of publicly available  sequences  across the entire genome and we can see  that it is quite diverse across the  entire genome  the hiv tat protein is encoded in two  exons across the virus in moderately  diverse areas when transcribed these two  exons  create a protein with roughly six  functional domains  that goes and does many activities of  the virus  its namesake the transactivator of  transcription  in this process hiv transcription  is often very leaky and not very  efficient  however when the tat is able to bind to  the tar  rna a very immature part one of the  first parts of viral transcripts  it stabilizes its complex with ptfe  allowing for longer transcripts to be  created  which in turn creates more tat which in  turn  creates more transcription creating a  positive feedback loop  which can allow this virus to escape  latency  cat has also been implicated in numerous  issues  in the neurocognitive implications  of hiv now the