ss a sequence  to find short patterns or phrases  we might think of these in the sequence  context as sequence  logos these are  weights of amino acids or nucleotides  that are responsible for a binding  we have recurrent networks these  networks  account for the order in which patterns  appear  by processing a sequence while  maintaining an  internal state that is updated as the  sequence is processed  so the two sentences really you liked it  and you really liked it while they both  contain the same  four words have distinctly opposite  meanings  recurrent network will be able to  capture that  and then in a sequence context we might  think of this as how complex 3d  structure  is important to understand whether  motifs will be on the inside or the  outside of a protein  now these two items together are often  blocked  and repeated to allow  one to extract more complex  patterns of patterns of patterns  and then those features are fed into a  densely connected network  and the purpose of these is to convert  the features  found previously into the actual  task specific predictions so this  is what allows a language model to  distinguish between sentences with  positive or negative sentiment  or in our context predicting  protein function from the sequences  now neural network can actually be  implemented with  minimal coding nowadays so using the pi  torch  neural network architecture we can  define the structure of a network  create the early embedding layers  create these recurrent blocks  produce this prediction structure  and then describe how data should flow  between each one of these blocks  in only about a hundred lines of code  now if we want to train a neural network  that can map  amino acid sequence to gene function we  need a database that has  that type of mapping and nicely the gene  ontology  database is a structured  vocabulary of gene functions  that are annotated across the unipro  database  which is a database of sequences  across many organisms so after filtering  for go terms that are rare and may not  be  easy have many observations we find that  there are roughly  500 000 sequences in the database  that contain these roughly 7 000 go  terms  after splitting them in order to have  both a training and validation cohort  we're still left with about 400 000  observations  now neural networks are trained by  structuring these mathematical graphs  passing data through and comparing  the output to the true  the true response variable and then  any differences are  updated in the weights through back  propagation  and gradient descent and these  sequences are passed through the network  over and over and over again  allowing it to extract as much data as  possible  as much knowledge as possible  so here i'm showing how the training  data  converges in the independent set of  validation data  reflects that it is able to distinguish  on proteins it has not seen before  now cross-entropy loss is a little bit  difficult to reason  about we often think about things as  the trade-off between sensitivity and  specificity in the class bar  called the area under the receiver  operator curve  now this number would be 1.0 if it was  perfect  and one-half if it was just randomly  guessing  uh and we can see that it is quite  accurate now not all go terms  are we able to predict as accurately  there are some that are more difficult  than others  these tend to be the rarer ones  and this work that i'm sort of  discussing here is built upon  some work on the deep go model first  published in 2018.  so just to go through how to read these  figures uh first  each row corresponds to a known  function of cat one of the 72 they're in  the gene ontology database  each column corresponds  to a specific pac patient  cat sequence uh they're about 6 000 of  them so we can't see each individual one  and then the color corresponds to how  likely the network believes that this  uh function should be associated with  the protein  now up 