ures of our face  as we add more and more  faces to this database it will  inherently become  more and more populated and have more  and more  false positives so in order to address  this  the facial recognition community came up  with a technique called  triplet loss now in triplet loss you  start with  two images of the same person  here two of will ferrell and one of an  unrelated person this is chad smith from  the red hot chili peppers  then we pass these through a neural  network that is designed  to convert these images into just  vectors of numbers  into just 128 number vector  then we adjust the neural network  so that the distances between the  vectors  that come from the faces of the same  person are closer  together and the distance between the  vectors of the unrelated person is  increased then as we feed  more and more faces through this  algorithm it will naturally  form clusters  and if we have an image for a person  that's in the database it will be  inside the cluster and if we have an  image for a person that has not been in  the database  they'll actually be distant from all  clusters  and neural networks trained with this  method are perfectly capable of  distinguishing between  will ferrell and chat smith when they  dress up as each other  now protein protein interaction  prediction can be framed as a triplet  loss problem  here in facial recognition we have two  pictures of person a  an unrelated person we can also  take in protein protein interactions to  part  protein binding partners and unrelated  protein  and using a database of about seven  thousand  human binding triplets like this  and the neural network that was already  constructed  in order to convert proteins into  vectors  we're able to train a neural network  that could predict  which two proteins interact with each  other  and find a very high degree of accuracy  so i took the hiv proteins and we have a  collection of  known uh interactions that's maintained  by the ncbi  that map known hiv and human protein  protein interactions  so i i took these proteins and i threw  them through the network  and i was kind of unimpressed with the  results  you see here that my accuracy  my area under the curve is you know  0.5.6.7  but this is because this neural network  has never seen  an hiv protein it's only ever seen human  proteins  and so it doesn't know what type of  proteins  these hiv ones will react so we can use  a process called  transfer learning in which we take the  network that was already trained  and provide it with a subset of new data  and allow it to continue its learning  and when we do this we find that we get  a drastic increase a recovery of our  ability to predict  hiv human protein interactions  so here this is a similar graph except  now  each row corresponds to a human protein  that tat interacts with uh but there are  about a thousand of them so we can't  show them all  again there's  a collection of these proteins here  that we're terming as constitutive  binders  that from all hiv patients  their tasks will always bind to these  genes  and we use gene ontology enrichment to  look at the functions of these genes  we find that they're primarily involved  in interfering with the immune system  if there's one thing the cat needs to do  for hiv  it's help it evade the immune system  and then down here we have a collection  of proteins  that were terming conditional binders so  not all  patients habitat that will bind to these  human proteins  now there are many interesting things uh  in these collections of proteins  but the one i'm going to talk about is  its effect on calcium dysregulation  so is able cat is able to bind  to a large number of proteins that are  involved  in ion homeostasis in particular  calcium dysregulation one particular one  that we're interested in our lab  is called grin1 which is the ncbi  gene id for the nmdma receptor  a common receptor found on neurons  uh we've actually developed a  r01 that's been looking at  how hiv tat  may be interfering with  the activity of the nmdma receptor  and dysregulating calcium causing  some of the neurological complications  of hiv  so lastly i want to talk about how we  can leverage generative networks  in order to do a unique variant  prediction  of hiv and i started this talk  thinking or describing that there are  roughly 36 million people  in the world living with hiv however  we've sequenced  barely a hundred thousand of them across  all of science but  the rest of this genetic variation is  not random  right there are things we know about hiv  sequence and can explain  it has some phylogeny relationships uh  there's difference between subtypes  across the world  and we know how art interferes with that  there are things we know but can't quite  explain  like how the structure to function  relationships are important  in uh in the sequence of the proteins  and how immune pressure may influence  that  and then there are things we don't know  and can't explain  so i want to talk about a different type  of neural network  one that can actually imagine new  things so the faces you're looking at  here  don't exist uh there are no people with  these faces they were  imagined by a computer from  a collection of random numbers  you can actually go to this person does  not  exist.com and get an infinite stream  of these faces  now not all excuse me not all of the  faces are perfect  uh this one here on the left you can see  that it doesn't understand  level of symmetry across faces  here you can see that it's particularly  poor at replicating jewelry  and sometimes it just puts people in  weird kaleidoscope worlds  in which they live with a whole bunch of  feet around them  so these images were generated  using a model called a generative  adversarial  neural network and these networks  are designed to take in random noise  and convert that into a random  object in another space so we'll take a  random set of numbers  and generate a random face that  corresponds  to those numbers and sometimes that face  has a weird person behind it  now it showed in the original papers  that this works for more than just faces  you could give it  birds or planes or bikes  or bedrooms or boats  you'll be able to create convincingly  real yet fake images of all of them  and then some work in 2017  showed that this would also be possible  to do with dna  so we took this and generated our  created our own gan  to look at hiv tax sequences  so these types of models we take noise  or literal random numbers uh  and we pass them into a black box that's  designed  through convolutional neural networks to  turn  those numbers into random letters  a's t's g's and c's  and then we combine that with a  collection of  true cat sequences and we have a whole  bunch of these  and then we create another convolutional  neural network  that is designed to distinguish  between real and fake sequences  so as the generator generates sequences  the critic will  evaluate those and it will guess which  ones are real and fake  but we know which ones are real and fake  so we can update the critic  and make it better at distinguishing  between real and fake sequences  and we can also update the generator  making it better at creating  convincingly fake sequences and then we  can  loop this over and over and over  allowing them  to compete in this way  and eventually you get to sequences that  you can't distinguish which ones are  real or fake  um but these uh  these neural networks sometimes collude  and they're also not perfect  here are two images that i took from  this person does not exist.com  the one on the right is clearly a face  the one on the left is clearly not a  face  but the neural network believed it was  so often the way to solve this problem  is to use some sort of generic  face detector that's not related to your  research and use it as  a check on what you see  now in the sequence world we would use  blast right i