d make  sure that the question you're trying to  answer  is one that is answerable with the data  that you have  so i'd like to thank my funding sources  uh this is my lab this was back in a  time where you could actually congregate  without masks  deborah here is my gene ontology wizard  and angie and bobby are  did the work on the early neural network  stuff  i also like to thank uh the participants  of the cares cohort that  uh that donated their sequences and all  the other researchers  of the 18th floor and the immid  and i want to close with one final slide  next up is josh earl we work together  uh on these types of projects and  i want to sort of give a lead in for his  as well  that these neural networks we have  trained to convert  sequence into protein function  now this means that this network has  learned the features that are important  for these protein functions  we can think of one of them as a feature  extractor and one of them is a predictor  and we can remove the predictor  and just look at the extracted features  and josh has talked we'll talk about how  those features can be useful in other  contexts  and he uses them to explore how  orthogonous genes may be related to each  other  thank you very much

